From f9ccb4afc7c033e6283c59ba32086efcf55b16ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 11:43:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05550/11362] [outer-loop] root_clutter_sweep.py: protect
 research-roadmap-next.yaml from the 30-min janitor

User: 'dig into that self healing.' Dispatched an Explore agent first, which
correctly ruled out any purpose-built stuck-roadmap detector in
research_conductor.py / exclusion_manifest_lint.py (live-verified zero
subprocess activity in the exact 2-minute window .476's stuck roadmap
vanished during) and traced the disappearance to an anonymous '[conductor]
Checkpoint: preserve uncommitted work' commit -- correctly identifying the
cause as external to the conductor process, but not yet identifying what.

Follow-up found it directly: scripts/root_clutter_sweep.py, run every 30
minutes by ~/.carnot/orphan-cleanup.sh (via carnot-orphan-cleanup.timer), is
a generic janitor that relocates any UNTRACKED root file older than 120
minutes to .root-scratch-trash/. research-roadmap-next.yaml is untracked by
nature exactly while stuck (it only becomes tracked once activation
succeeds) -- precisely the state a stall produces. /tmp/root-clutter-
sweep.log shows the smoking gun directly ('mv research-roadmap-next.yaml',
at least twice); confirmed physically on disk at .root-scratch-trash/
2026-07-02/research-roadmap-next.yaml and .root-scratch-trash/2026-07-03/
research-roadmap-next.yaml. There was no self-healing mechanism -- every
stall was silently discarding up to 2 hours of real planner compute rather
than surviving long enough to get diagnosed and fixed.

Fix: added research-roadmap-next.yaml to ALLOWLIST alongside its already-
protected siblings (research-roadmap.yaml, research-complete.yaml) -- it
was simply missed when the allowlist was built, likely because it's
transiently untracked by design unlike its always-tracked siblings.
'Transiently untracked' isn't 'safe to delete.'

4 new tests (tests/python/test_root_clutter_sweep_roadmap_protection.py)
exercise the real sweep() function end-to-end (REPO monkeypatched to an
isolated tmp_path, not just an ALLOWLIST membership assertion, so a future
refactor of the matching logic can't silently re-break protection while
membership still trivially passes): a stuck old untracked draft survives a
real --apply sweep; an unrelated old untracked scratch .py file is still
swept (confirms the fix doesn't over-protect); a fresh young draft is left
alone regardless (confirms the age guard's own in-flight protection is
unaffected).

Complementary with the earlier same-day _prose_addresses_prior fix
(scripts/exclusion_manifest_lint.py): that fix reduces how often a stall
happens; this fix ensures that when one happens for any other reason, the
stuck work survives long enough to actually get fixed instead of silently
vanishing.

